The Red run exposed a boundary-semantics defect in the Phase 1 resolver:
stepToNextHeel lands exactly ON a vertex, but the resolver treated that as
still inside the previous segment, so no heel crossing was ever detected from
that method - no onTurn, no announcement, no checkpoint.

Resolved by separating two concerns the original code conflated:
  - heel crossing: boundaries b with from < b <= to. Arrival counts.
  - segment index: segmented uses >= boundary, non-segmented uses > boundary.

The asymmetry is not arbitrary - it mirrors the two pointer paths. Segmented
pointer confirms a checkpoint at projection.t >= 0.98 (arrival), while
non-segmented pointer advances only at nextProj.t > 0 (strictly past). Keeping
non-segmented conservative is load-bearing: on the [100,100,5] track, resting
exactly on the final vertex reaches 97.56% progress, and treating that as
having entered the final segment would grant unlock with none of it traversed
- the defect 6c8c6fb closed, re-entering through the stepping door.

onCheckpoint fires with the heel index and progress, matching the pointer
signature. onTurn fires once per heel even when one step spans several.
